UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 8: Dance elements and technique', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a chosen ending rather than a mistake. The beginning introduces the intent. The middle develops it, often with a change of level or direction. The end resolves it. Holding a clear shape tells the audience the phrase is finished. Stopping because the next step was forgotten is not the same as choosing stillness. You can mark the three parts on paper.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA phrase about arriving home starts with a walk and continues with a turn, then the dancer simply leaves the space. How would you rewrite the ending so stillness is a choice, and what jobs do the beginning and the middle still need?\n\nFEEDBACK\nHold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness. The beginning should show the first sign of arriving, and the middle should develop it with a change of level or direction. Leaving without a held shape makes the stop look accidental. Stillness works as an ending when the shape and the focus stay readable.\n\nWATCH FOR\nA phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed. Stillness is a movement choice with its own duration. A held ending can be the strongest close. Endless motion can make a phrase feel unfinished.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan a remembering phrase that changes level or direction in the middle and uses stillness on purpose at the end. Say how many counts each part lasts. Do not copy the searching phrase. Performing it is optional.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Dance","learning_intention":"A phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a chosen ending rather than a mistake. The beginning introduces the intent. The middle develops it, often with a change of level or direction. The end resolves it. Holding a clear shape tells the audience the phrase is finished. Stopping because the next step was forgotten is not the same as choosing stillness. You can mark the three parts on paper.","lesson_number":8,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DA4-PER-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan a remembering phrase that changes level or direction in the middle and uses stillness on purpose at the end. Say how many counts each part lasts. Do not copy the searching phrase. Performing it is optional. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a phrase on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed.. Correction: Hold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed. Stillness is a movement choice with its own duration. A held ending can be the strongest close. Endless motion can make a phrase feel unfinished. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a phrase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a phrase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a phrase with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a phrase. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a phrase. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","Hold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness"],"question":"A phrase about arriving home starts with a walk and continues with a turn, then the dancer simply leaves the space. How would you rewrite the ending so stillness is a choice, and what jobs do the beginning and the middle still need?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a phrase; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a chosen ending rather than a mistake","Hold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness","Careful use of a beginning with clear evidence","A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a beginning, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply a phrase to new material and name the a beginning used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed","Name a beginning only, without linking it to a phrase"],"question":"Which response best applies a phrase with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on a phrase. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed","Hold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for a phrase","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A still sideways look begins the searching phrase and makes the intent readable","A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed","Name a beginning only, with no linked example","Plan a remembering phrase that changes level or direction in the middle and uses stillness on purpose at the end","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a phrase to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a phrase with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a phrase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a phrase on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a phrase. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a phrase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed.. Correction: Hold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-dance-y9-w04-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Dance 7–10 (2023) outcome DA4-PER-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Movement is optional, pain-free, and adult-supervised. Written plans are acceptable evidence.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a phrase.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use a phrase with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a beginning and explain why it matters.","I can practise a middle independently on a new prompt.","I can notice ending stillness needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a chosen ending rather than a mistake.","options":["A phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a chosen ending rather than a mistake","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA still sideways look begins the searching phrase and makes the intent readable. The middle travels for four counts and changes from middle height to low. A held low reach, with the eyes still focused, ends the phrase on purpose. A weak version wanders off and drops the arms halfway, so the finish looks accidental.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA phrase about arriving home starts with a walk and continues with a turn, then the dancer simply leaves the space. How would you rewrite the ending so stillness is a choice, and what jobs do the beginning and the middle still need?\n\nFEEDBACK\nHold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness. The beginning should show the first sign of arriving, and the middle should develop it with a change of level or direction. Leaving without a held shape makes the stop look accidental. Stillness works as an ending when the shape and the focus stay readable.","explain":"A phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a chosen ending rather than a mistake. The beginning introduces the intent. The middle develops it, often with a change of level or direction. The end resolves it. Holding a clear shape tells the audience the phrase is finished. Stopping because the next step was forgotten is not the same as choosing stillness. You can mark the three parts on paper.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed. Stillness is a movement choice with its own duration. A held ending can be the strongest close. Endless motion can make a phrase feel unfinished. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast an accidental stop, with dropped arms, and a chosen ending that holds a readable shape.","caption":"Stillness is an ending choice when the shape stays readable. Dropping the shape makes the stop look accidental.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Accidental stop</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Arms drop and focus leaves</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Chosen stillness</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Hold a readable end shape</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: A still sideways look begins the searching phrase and makes the intent readable. The middle travels for four counts and  Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: A still sideways look begins the searching phrase and makes the intent readable. The middle travels for four counts and  Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA still sideways look begins the searching phrase and makes the intent readable. The middle travels for four counts and changes from middle height to low. A held low reach, with the eyes still focused, ends the phrase on purpose. A weak version wanders off and drops the arms halfway, so the finish looks accidental. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels show a beginning that opens the intent and a middle that develops the idea before an ending resolves it.","caption":"A phrase has a beginning that introduces, a middle that develops, and an end that resolves.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Phrase beginning</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Opens the intent clearly</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Middle and end</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Develops, then resolves it</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed. Stillness is a movement choice with its own duration. A held ending can be the strongest close. Endless motion can make a phrase feel unfinished. Here is a more helpful example: A still sideways look begins the searching phrase and makes the intent readable. The middle travels for four counts and changes from middle height to low. A held low reach, with the eyes still focused, ends the phrase on purpose. A weak version wanders off and drops the arms halfway, so the finish looks accidental.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHold a clear doorway shape for two counts so the arrival reads as chosen stillness. The beginning should show the first sign of arriving, and the middle should develop it with a change of level or direction. Leaving without a held shape makes the stop look accidental. Stillness works as an ending when the shape and the focus stay readable.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. A phrase must keep moving until the music ends, because any stillness is taken to mean the dancer failed. Stillness is a movement choice with its own duration. A held ending can be the strongest close. Endless motion can make a phrase feel unfinished. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a phrase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA phrase needs a beginning, a middle and an end, and stillness can be a chosen ending rather than a mistake. The beginning introduces the intent. The middle develops it, often with a change of level or direction. The end resolves it. Holding a clear shape tells the audience the phrase is finished. Stopping because the next step was forgotten is not the same as choosing stillness. You can mark the three parts on paper. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 8: Dance elements and technique","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Dance elements and technique","week":6,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA still sideways look begins the searching phrase and makes the intent readable. The middle travels for four counts and changes from middle height to low. A held low reach, with the eyes still focused, ends the phrase on purpose. A weak version wanders off and drops the arms halfway, so the finish looks accidental.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast an accidental stop, with dropped arms, and a chosen ending that holds a readable shape.","caption":"Stillness is an ending choice when the shape stays readable. Dropping the shape makes the stop look accidental.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Accidental stop</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Arms drop and focus leaves</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Chosen stillness</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Hold a readable end shape</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two panels show a beginning that opens the intent and a middle that develops the idea before an ending resolves it.","caption":"A phrase has a beginning that introduces, a middle that develops, and an end that resolves.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Phrase beginning</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Opens the intent clearly</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Middle and end</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Develops, then resolves it</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-dance-y9-w04-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
